    <section class="page-section"><div class="page-header"><h1>PROJECTS</h1><p class="page-subtitle">Python projects built in CMU CS Academy. You can play each one directly below.</p></div><div class="projects-grid">
    <div class="project-card"><div class="project-header"><h2>BOMB DETECTION GAME</h2><span class="project-tag">Python</span></div><p class="project-description">A suspense-based exploration game made in CMU CS Academy. The player presses B to start, moves with WASD, follows coordinates, uses a mouse-controlled flashlight, and tries to find the bomb before time runs out.</p><div class="project-details"><h3>Play the Game</h3><iframe width="410" height="460" src="https://academy.cs.cmu.edu/sharing/wheatGoat831899/embed"></iframe><h3>Challenges and Solutions</h3><ul><li><strong>Movement:</strong> I used velocity, acceleration, speed limits, and deceleration to make the player movement feel smoother.</li><li><strong>Flashlight:</strong> I used arcs and angle calculations so the light follows the player and points toward the mouse.</li><li><strong>Game state:</strong> I used variables for the bomb timer, active state, explosion, sound, and room coordinates.</li></ul><h3>Lessons Learned</h3><ul><li>Complex games need separate systems for movement, collision, timing, sound, and screen changes.</li><li>Testing matters because one collision or timing problem can affect the whole game.</li></ul><h3>Code Features</h3><p>Groups, collision detection, sound, timers, mouse tracking, keyboard input, random walls, velocity, deceleration, coordinates, and visual effects.</p></div></div>
    <div class="project-card"><div class="project-header"><h2>DON'T CLICK PLEASE</h2><span class="project-tag">Python</span></div><p class="project-description">An interactive reaction project made in CMU CS Academy. The program starts with a warning not to click. When the user clicks, the screen becomes a chaotic star animation with randomized colors, movement, rotation, and new stars.</p><div class="project-details"><h3>Play the Game</h3><iframe width="410" height="460" src="https://academy.cs.cmu.edu/sharing/orangeHamster358378/embed"></iframe><h3>Challenges and Solutions</h3><ul><li><strong>Object creation:</strong> I used a list of stars and added new child stars during the animation.</li><li><strong>Random movement:</strong> Each star has its own dx and dy values so the animation feels unpredictable.</li><li><strong>Boundaries:</strong> Stars bounce when they hit the edge by reversing direction.</li></ul><h3>Lessons Learned</h3><ul><li>Lists make it possible to control many objects at once.</li><li>Randomness can make a simple program feel more dynamic and creative.</li><li>Animation requires repeatedly updating objects inside onStep.</li></ul><h3>Code Features</h3><p>Lists, for loops, random numbers, mouse press events, step events, dx/dy movement, rotation, color changes, and object spawning.</p></div></div>
    <div class="project-card"><div class="project-header"><h2>CROSSING THE STREET</h2><span class="project-tag">Python</span></div><p class="project-description">A game where the player moves across the street, avoids moving cars, collects a coin, and returns home to win. If the player touches a car or leaves the screen, the game resets.</p><div class="project-details"><h3>Play the Game</h3><iframe width="410" height="460" src="https://academy.cs.cmu.edu/sharing/oliveGiraffe1105/embed"></iframe><h3>Challenges and Solutions</h3><ul><li><strong>Moving obstacles:</strong> Cars move up and down and reverse direction when they reach the screen edge.</li><li><strong>Collision detection:</strong> The player resets when touching a car but collects the coin when touching the coin.</li><li><strong>Win condition:</strong> The game checks whether the coin has been collected before allowing the player to win at home.</li></ul><h3>Lessons Learned</h3><ul><li>Games need clear rules for losing, winning, and resetting.</li><li>Boolean variables are useful for tracking whether an objective is complete.</li><li>Collision detection is one of the most important parts of interactive games.</li></ul><h3>Code Features</h3><p>Keyboard input, moving rectangles, collision detection, reset function, booleans, and a win label.</p></div></div>
    <div class="project-card"><div class="project-header"><h2>BREAD OVEN GAME</h2><span class="project-tag">Python</span></div><p class="project-description">An oven simulation where the player puts bread into the oven, holds a key to bake it, and must take it out before it burns. This project was built to practice groups, dx/dy movement, for loops, and onStep events.</p><div class="project-details"><h3>Play the Game</h3><iframe width="410" height="460" src="https://academy.cs.cmu.edu/sharing/cornflowerBlueShark9801/embed"></iframe><h3>Challenges and Solutions</h3><ul><li><strong>Grouped shapes:</strong> I built the bread and oven from multiple shapes grouped together.</li><li><strong>Timed baking:</strong> I used a howBaked variable that increases when the player holds the baking key.</li><li><strong>Changing state:</strong> The bread changes from tan to brown when baked, then black if it burns.</li></ul><h3>Lessons Learned</h3><ul><li>Groups make it easier to move and rotate shapes together.</li><li>Booleans help control game stages like moving, baked, burnt, and take out.</li><li>Small simulations can still use important programming ideas.</li></ul><h3>Code Features</h3><p>Groups, dx movement, for loops, step events, key presses, key holds, state variables, and visual feedback.</p></div></div>
